## Decoding the question
Three tasks. "**What is patriarchy?**" — a precise definition, with the main theorists. "**Critically compare**" the three schools — explain each, then compare **point by point** (cause, site, remedy, thinkers, weaknesses); a table or figure helps. "**Which best accounts for … Pakistan**" — a reasoned **judgement**, tested against Pakistani evidence.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Patriarchy** — literally "rule of the father" — is a social system in which men hold power over women in the family, economy, politics, religion and culture, and in which this power is presented as natural.

#### ii) The argument
**Radical feminists** see patriarchy as the oldest and deepest oppression, rooted in control of women's bodies; **Marxist feminists** see it as a product of **capitalism and private property**; **socialist feminists** see **two linked systems** working together. In Pakistan, where women's subordination is both **material** (property, work, wages) and **bodily** (honour, violence, marriage), the socialist-feminist account — sharpened by radical insights on violence — explains most.

### 2. Patriarchy defined

#### i) Millett
**Kate Millett** (*Sexual Politics*, ==1970==) made patriarchy the key concept of feminism: "sexual politics" — power relations between the sexes — run through family, religion, literature and force.

#### ii) Walby
**Sylvia Walby** (*Theorizing Patriarchy*, ==1990==) identified **six structures**: paid work, household production, culture, sexuality, male violence and the state — and distinguished **private** patriarchy (in the home) from **public** patriarchy (in work and the state).

#### iii) Kandiyoti
**Deniz Kandiyoti** ("Bargaining with Patriarchy", ==1988==) described "**classic patriarchy**" in the belt from North Africa to South Asia, where young brides gain power later as mothers of sons and mothers-in-law — a "**patriarchal bargain**".

### 3. Radical feminism: patriarchy as the root

#### i) The core claim
"Radical" comes from the Latin ***radix***, **root**. Patriarchy is the **oldest and most universal** oppression, older than class or capitalism, and it operates above all through the **family, sexuality, the body and violence**.

#### ii) The thinkers

##### (a) Millett and Firestone
Millett located patriarchy in culture and force; **Shulamith Firestone** (*The Dialectic of Sex*, ==1970==) located its root in **biology and reproduction**, calling women a "**sex class**".

##### (b) Brownmiller and MacKinnon
**Susan Brownmiller** (*Against Our Will*, ==1975==) argued that **rape** keeps all women in fear; **Catharine MacKinnon** (==1979==) showed that **sexual harassment** is sex discrimination.

#### iii) The slogan and the method
"**The personal is political**" (Carol Hanisch, ==1969–70==), and **consciousness-raising** in women-only groups.

### 4. Radical feminism: strengths and weaknesses

#### i) Strengths
It exposed **violence, rape and harassment** as political, not private, and produced **rape crisis centres**, **women's shelters** and harassment law.

#### ii) Weaknesses
It treats all women as one, ignoring **class and race** (bell hooks, Audre Lorde); Firestone's biology risks **determinism**; and a universal patriarchy struggles to explain **change**.

### 5. Marxist feminism: capitalism and property

#### i) Engels
**Friedrich Engels** (*The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State*, ==1884==) argued that with **private property** men wanted heirs, so **monogamy was imposed on women** — "the **world historical defeat of the female sex**".

#### ii) Housework as labour
Marxist feminists showed that women's **unpaid domestic labour** reproduces the worker for capital: **Margaret Benston** (==1969==), **Mariarosa Dalla Costa and Selma James** (==1972==) and **Silvia Federici** (==1975==) — "They say it is love. We say it is unwaged work."

#### iii) The remedy
End private property and capitalism; bring women into paid work and **socialise care**.

### 6. Marxist feminism: strengths and weaknesses

#### i) Strengths
It made **unpaid work** visible and explained women's role as a cheap **reserve army** of labour.

#### ii) Weaknesses
It is "**sex-blind**": it reduces gender to class, cannot explain why **men** of all classes dominate women, and underplays violence and sexuality. Socialist states left women with a **double burden**.

### 7. Socialist feminism: two systems together

#### i) The core claim
Women's oppression results from **capitalism and patriarchy working together**, in paid work **and** the family.

#### ii) The thinkers

##### (a) Mitchell
**Juliet Mitchell** (*Woman's Estate*, ==1971==) identified four structures: **production, reproduction, sexuality** and the **socialisation of children**.

##### (b) Hartmann
**Heidi Hartmann** ("The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism", ==1979==) called Marxism sex-blind and proposed **dual systems**; male unions' demand for a "**family wage**" pushed women home.

##### (c) Eisenstein, Young and Mies
**Zillah Eisenstein** spoke of "**capitalist patriarchy**"; **Iris Marion Young** (==1980==) argued for a **unified** system; **Maria Mies** (==1986==) described "**housewifisation**".

#### iii) The remedy
Change **both** the economy and the family — economic independence, socialised care, and an end to male privilege at home.

### 8. Socialist feminism: strengths and weaknesses

#### i) Strengths
It joins **class and gender**, paid and unpaid work, and explains the **double burden** — Arlie Hochschild's "**second shift**" (==1989==).

#### ii) Weaknesses
The link between the two systems is hard to specify, and it can still underplay **violence and sexuality**, the radical feminists' strongest ground.

::: fig compare | Three explanations of patriarchy compared.
@vs vs
## Radical
- Root: patriarchy itself
- Site: body, sexuality, family, violence
- Remedy: uproot patriarchy
## Marxist and socialist
- Root: capitalism (Marxist); capitalism + patriarchy (socialist)
- Site: paid and unpaid work, property, family
- Remedy: change the economy and the family
:::

### 9. The three compared

#### i) Cause
Radical: patriarchy itself; Marxist: capitalism and property; socialist: both together.

#### ii) Site
Radical: body, sexuality and violence; Marxist: the economy; socialist: the economy and the family.

#### iii) Who benefits
Radical: men as a group; Marxist: capital; socialist: capital **and** men.

#### iv) Remedy
Radical: transform culture and sexuality; Marxist: abolish capitalism; socialist: change production and reproduction together.

### 10. Testing them on Pakistan

#### i) Evidence for the radical account
**Honour killing** (HRCP recorded at least **405** in ==2024==), domestic violence (**28%** of ever-married women since age 15, PDHS ==2017–18==), forced marriage and control of women's mobility show patriarchy operating through **the body and honour**, across classes.

#### ii) Evidence for the Marxist account
Most employed women work in **agriculture**, much of it as **unpaid family helpers**; **home-based workers** stitch for piece rates; women's labour-force participation is **24.4%** (LFS ==2024–25==). Women's work is cheap because it is treated as secondary.

#### iii) Evidence for the socialist account
Property and patriarchy are fused: women are denied **inheritance** to keep land in the male line; *karo-kari* accusations are used to **grab land**; marriage within the clan protects property. Kandiyoti's **classic patriarchy** describes this joint system exactly.

### 11. My answer

#### i) Socialist feminism explains most
Pakistan's patriarchy is both **economic** — land, inheritance, unpaid and cheap labour — and **social** — honour, marriage, seclusion. Only an account that joins **capitalism (and feudal property) with patriarchy** captures this fusion.

#### ii) Sharpened by radical insights
The socialist account must borrow the radical feminists' focus on **violence and the body**: honour killing and harassment are not reducible to economics.

#### iii) Spoken in local terms
Pakistani movements have succeeded when they framed these insights in **constitutional and Islamic** language — the 1948 inheritance law and the 2016 honour law are examples.

### 12. Conclusion

#### i) The comparison
Radical feminism explains patriarchy's grip on the **body**; Marxist feminism explains its use of **unpaid and cheap labour**; socialist feminism explains how the two **reinforce each other**.

#### ii) The verdict
For Pakistan, where land, honour and labour are bound together, a **socialist-feminist account sharpened by radical insights** best explains women's subordination — and points to remedies in both the economy and the family.

## Why this answer scores
- Defines patriarchy with **three theorists** (Millett, Walby, Kandiyoti).
- Explains **each school** with its thinkers and dates, then weighs strengths and weaknesses.
- Compares them **point by point** (cause, site, beneficiary, remedy) with a **compare** figure.
- **Tests** each account on Pakistani evidence before reaching a reasoned verdict.
